634 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 79 0 R
/T (Q10. Critically discuss the statement: "A high-performing Machine Learning model is not necessarily a successful Machine Learning system." Explain the technical and practical factors that should be considered beyond model accuracy. )
/E (Q10. Critically discuss the statement: "A high-performing Machine Learning model is not necessarily a successful Machine Learning system." Explain the technical and practical factors that should be considered beyond model accuracy. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
635 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (The statement is largely true. A model is only one component of an ML system. A model that scores very well on a test set may still fail to deliver value once it is placed in the real world, because a system also includes data pipelines, infrastructure, users, business processes, monitoring and people. Success means the system reliably solves the real problem, safely and at acceptable cost, over time. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
636 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 79 0 R
/T (Why Accuracy Alone Is Not Enough )
/E (Why Accuracy Alone Is Not Enough )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
637 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (Accuracy can be misleading. In a fraud dataset with 1% fraud, a model that predicts "genuine" for every transaction is 99% accurate, but completely useless. Test data may also not represent real- world data, so good offline results may not carry over to production. A well-known illustration is the Netflix Prize: the winning ensemble improved the prediction error, but Netflix reported that it was not deployed because the engineering effort needed was not justified by the gain. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
